{
  "id": "SGB-DUAL-60",
  "title_pt": "Cenario sintetico de 60 nos - 15 sites com duplo acesso",
  "title_en": "Synthetic 60-node scenario - 15 dual-homed grid sites",
  "provenance": "SYNTHETIC MODEL. Two independent access networks reach the same 15 grid sites: a private LTE star, with two eNodeBs and a short relay tier, and a 900 MHz store-and-forward mesh fed by fibre from the operations centre. Each site's CPE and 900 MHz remote radio cable into that site's edge router, which is the smart-grid boundary and the only point downstream where the two networks meet. The edge router is a STUB: a route may begin or end there but never cross it, because a grid site is customer edge and does not carry a neighbouring site's backhaul. Access link quality falls off with distance - sharply for LTE, which collapses at cell edge, and gently for 900 MHz, which propagates further; that contrast is the engineering reason both networks exist. Backhaul links use fixed directional antennas and do not degrade with distance. Identifiers, coordinates and link qualities are invented for this exercise and contain nothing from any laboratory's inventory, addressing or configuration.",
  "coordinate_system": "Local planar grid in metres; origin at the operations centre. Distances are Euclidean and DERIVED from these coordinates, which is what keeps a straight-line heuristic admissible.",
